So your organization is called the Center for Open Science, and I'm hoping you can explain for our listeners, many of whom are not psychologists. In fact, most of whom are not psychologists, what you mean by that? How does it relate to the reproducibility problem that we're talking about right now? Yeah. So the Center for Open Science is a nonprofit technology and culture change organization that has a mission to increase openness, integrity, and reproducibility of research. And it spun out of my lab in 2013.
所以你们的组织叫做开放科学中心，我希望你能为我们的听众解释一下这个名字的含义——我们的许多听众并不是心理学家，事实上，其中大多数都不是心理学家。这与我们现在讨论的可重复性问题有什么关系？是的。开放科学中心是一个非营利性的技术与文化变革组织，其使命是提高研究的开放性、完整性和可重复性。它于2013年从我的实验室独立出来。

14
00:09:18.000 --> 00:10:02.000
Jeff Spees and I launched the organization. Our goal was to say to recognize that this is a culture challenge. This isn't a matter of just teaching people how to do it. research in a better way, having them read Uri Leif and Joe's paper and say, okay, don't do those things and your research will be fine. That's helpful, right? We need education and training about how to do the best research we can. But really, as a researcher in the field, I have two major constraints that make it so that the work that I do might be less credible than I want it to be. One is the limitations of my own mind.
杰夫·斯皮斯（Jeff Spees）和我共同创立了这个组织。我们的目标是认识到这是一个文化挑战。这不仅仅是教导人们如何以更好的方式开展研究，让他们阅读乌里、莱夫和乔的论文，然后说：“好了，别做那些事，你的研究就会没问题。”这当然是有帮助的，对吧？我们需要关于如何开展最佳研究的教育和培训。但事实上，作为该领域的연구人员，我有两个主要的制约因素，这使得我所做的工作可能不如我希望的那样可信。首先是我自己思维的局限性。

15
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:22.000
I intend to do good work. I intend to find out things that are true. But I have skin in the game. If I find something super exciting, that advances my career. I'm more likely to get a job. I'm more likely to get tenure at my institution. I'm more likely to get awards. And if I find things that don't work a lot and things that are kind of messy,
我打算做好工作，我打算找出真实的事情。但我在其中有切身利益。如果我发现了一些超级令人兴奋的事情，这会推进我的职业生涯。我更有可能找到工作，更有可能在我的机构获得终身教职，更有可能获得奖项。而如果我发现了很多行不通的事情，或者发现事情一团糟，

16
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:53.000
well, that's not going to do as much for my career. So I have a conflict of interest with my own results. And so I may bring to bear lots of not reasons, but rationalizations for how it is I can justify reporting this finding rather than that finding. Why it is I would take these two studies that looked really good for my phenomenon and decide that those four studies that didn't find evidence for it were actually just bad studies. So I shouldn't report them at all.
嗯，这对我的职业生涯就没有那么大的帮助了。因此，我对自己的研究结果存在利益冲突。所以，对于如何证明自己报告这个发现而不是那个发现是正当的，我可能会找出许多并非理由的理由来进行合理化解释。为什么我会挑出这两项看起来对我的现象非常有利的研究，却决定那四项没有找到证据的研究其实都是糟糕的研究，因此我根本不应该报告它们。

17
00:10:53.000 --> 00:11:40.000
So the bias that I bring to the table, I may not recognize in how it is I do my work. And so we have that constraint is how do we help ourselves be more credible by being more transparent and confront our own biases, just like we study biases about how we make judgments of other people in everyday life and implicit bias. Same sorts of principles about scientific evidence. The second major constraint is the structure of the system, how it is that I'm rewarded, right? Because I need to get those exciting findings, though that constraint, that part of the structure of the system, what universities do to decide who to hire and who to promote, what journals
因此，我带进研究中的偏见，可能在自己做事的方式中并未意识到。所以我们面临的制约因素是：我们如何通过更加透明来帮助自己变得更可信，并直面我们自己的偏见？正如我们在日常生活中研究关于如何对他人做出判断的偏见以及内隐偏见一样。科学证据也同样适用这些原则。第二个主要制约因素是系统的结构，也就是我如何获得奖励，对吧？因为我需要获得那些令人兴奋的发现——系统的这一结构性部分，即大学如何决定聘用谁和晋升谁，期刊

18
00:11:40.000 --> 00:12:18.000
do to decide which findings to publish, what funders do decide who to give grants to, those create a system that constrains my behavior as much as I want to do things well. In order to be in that system, I have to align to some degree to those reward systems. So what the Center for Open Science exists to do is to confront both of these and try to facilitate a change in the research culture. And it does so by providing technology for researchers to make it easy to be more transparent, to show you what my plans were in advance, to show all of the things that I found,
如何决定发表哪些发现，资助者如何决定将资助给谁——这些创造了一个系统，无论我多么想把事情做好，它都在约束着我的行为。为了在这个系统中生存，我必须在某种程度上顺应那些奖励系统。因此，开放科学中心存在的目的，就是直面这两者，并努力促进研究文化的变革。它通过提供技术来帮助研究人员轻松做到更加透明：提前展示我的计划是什么，展示我发现的所有事情，

19
00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:46.000
not putting anything, hiding anything away, to making my data available to you to reanalyze it. And we work on the structure, working with publishers and funders and institutions, to try to nudge those policies and incentives so that what researchers are rewarded for is doing rigorous, transparent research, rather than producing exciting findings that may not be credible.
不隐瞒任何东西，让我的数据可供你重新分析。我们致力于结构层面的改革，与出版商、资助者和机构合作，试图调整这些政策和激励措施，以便研究人员因开展严谨、透明的研究而获得奖励，而不是因产生可能不可信的激动人心的发现而获得奖励。

