00:12:46.000 --> 00:13:39.000
Your center encourages several things, open data, open materials, and pre-registration of studies. What are each of those practices and how are they helping with science being more credible and more transparent? So with open data, if you want to be able to analyze my data to see if I found, what I said I found, simple reproduction, you can't do that unless you have access to my data. If you'd like to see whether my findings are robust, if you change an analyst this way, analysis that way, all different reasonable choices you might make and see if the same findings recur, you need access to the data. Or if you want to combine the data I generated with similar kinds of data by many other researchers to see how robust is the phenomenon when you look in Virginia,
你们的中心鼓励几项事情：开放数据、开放材料和研究预注册。这些做法分别是什么？它们如何帮助科学变得更加可信和透明？关于开放数据，如果你想能够分析我的数据，看看我是否发现了、我所说我发现的东西，即简单的重复，除非你能访问我的数据，否则你无法做到这一点。如果你想看看我的发现是否稳健，如果你以这种方式或那种方式改变分析（你可能会做出各种不同的合理选择），并看看是否会出现同样的发现，你需要访问数据。或者，如果你想把我生成的数据与许多其他研究人员的类似数据结合起来，看看当你审视弗吉尼亚州时，

21
00:13:39.000 --> 00:14:17.000
where I am and participants in Iowa and participants in Israel and participants in Russia, then you need to be able to aggregate that data. And you can do some of that metanalytically with just the outcomes, but it's even more powerful to be able to look at the raw data in aggregate. So that open data is part of where we started earlier when you asked about the peer review process, is if we want to be able to self-correct effectively and assess credibility, having access to the original data is very useful. For open materials, you want to see how is it that I generated that data.
也就是我所在的地方，以及爱荷华州的参与者、以色列的参与者和俄罗斯的参与者时，这种现象有多么稳健，那么你需要能够聚合这些数据。你可以仅通过结果进行某种元分析（meta-analytical），但如果能够将原始数据聚合起来看，则会更强大。因此，开放数据正是我们早些时候谈到同行评议过程时的起点：如果我们想要有效地进行自我纠正并评估可信度，访问原始数据是非常有用的。对于开放材料，你想看看我是如何生成这些数据的。

22
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:42.000
You want to know what was the exact format of the survey or the behavioral intervention that I did or what it is that the people did in the setting when I was trying to elicit their behavior. If you don't have access to all of the actual content of the study, how the study was conducted, it's hard for you to then assess the credibility of the data. It's also hard for you to say, you know what?
你想知道调查的确切格式是什么，或者我所做的行为干预是什么，或者当我试图引发出人们的行为时，他们在那种环境下做了什么。如果你无法访问研究的所有实际内容、研究是如何进行的，你就很难评估数据的可信度。你也很难说：“你知道吗？

23
00:14:42.000 --> 00:15:12.000
I want to know if that replicates in this other context. Sure, he showed it in his lab under these very limited conditions, but really if that's meaningful evidence, it should also occur in a corporate environment or it should occur in everyday life in some way. And so I want to test it over in this population or that population. Not having access to materials makes it harder for you to assess how it is I did it and then to redo it. So sharing that, again, provides better, effective,
我想知道这在其他语境下是否能重复出来。”当然，他在极其有限的条件下在自己的实验室里展示了这一点，但如果这确实是有意义的证据，它也应该发生在企业环境中，或者应该以某种方式发生在日常生活中。因此，我想在这个群体或那个群体中对其进行测试。无法访问材料会使你更难评估我是如何做到这一点的，进而更难将其重做。因此，分享这些材料同样提供了更好、更有效的

24
00:15:12.000 --> 00:16:01.000
self-correction, extension, and building on each other's evidence. The last one, pre-registration, has two important roles. The first, what pre-registration means is when I plan to do a study, I write down some information about what the study is going to be and how I'm going to analyze the data, and I post that in an independent repository, so that in principle, anyone can discover all of the studies that I plan to do. And so the two problems that solves is, first, the publication bias. We talked about earlier where I do 10 studies, and I only show you the three that worked. Well, now, if I've pre-registered all of my studies in advance, you can find the other seven. you can say, oh, wait a second, Brian.
自我纠正、扩展以及在彼此证据基础上的构建。最后一点，预注册，具有两个重要的作用。首先，预注册的意思是，当我计划做一项研究时，我写下关于该研究将是什么以及我将如何分析数据的一些信息，并将其发布在一个独立的存储库中，以便原则上任何人都可以发现我计划做的所有研究。因此，它解决的两个问题是：首先是发表偏差。我们之前讨论过我做了10项研究，却只给你展示了起作用的3项。现在，如果我提前预注册了我所有的研究，你就能找到另外7项，

25
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Is the evidence as credible as you say it is? Like, what happened to these? And I say, oh, no, no, no. Those were bad studies, you know, and you might agree or you might disagree. But at least you can see the evidence. The other reason for pre-registration is that it makes very clear what was planned beforehand and what I discovered after the fact. And both of those modes of research are super important.
你就可以说：“哦，等一下，布赖恩，这些证据真的像你说的那么可信吗？这些研究怎么了？”然后我说：“哦，不，不，不，那些都是糟糕的研究，你知道的。”你可能会同意，也可能会不同意。但至少你能看到这些证据。预注册的另一个原因是，它让人非常清楚地看出什么是事先计划好的，什么是事后发现的。这两种研究模式都超级重要。

26
00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:56.000
We have confirmatory modes of research where it is I have a hypothesis in advance. This theory makes these kinds of predictions. And so I'm going to collect some data to test the viability of my theory. See where it holds up. See where it's weak. And then revise our confidence in the theory as a consequence. That confirmatory part, it's really important to say in advance, how are you going to test it? And so then afterwards, I don't say, oh, actually my prediction is a little bit different.
我们有验证性的研究模式，也就是我事先有一个假设。这个理论做出了这些预测，因此我将收集一些数据来测试我的理论的可行性，看看它在哪里经得起检验，在哪里薄弱，并因此修正我们对该理论的信心。对于验证性部分，事先说明你将如何进行测试是非常重要的。这样在事后，我就不会说：“哦，其实我的预测有点不同，

27
00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:39.000
and I think it only alternatives. So I don't do that sort of reconstruction after the fact. The other part of that is that the evidence itself becomes easier to assess because then once I've seen the data, I am free to go into it and explore and discover new things and find out what's in the data that might shape new ideas, might generate hypotheses for me to test next. And that's where a lot of the new discovery in science occurs, right? It's not from what we're testing from existing hypotheses or theories. It's when the data go totally awry and send us in a whole new direction.
而且我认为只有其他选择。”所以我不会进行那种事后的重构。这的另一个部分是，证据本身变得更容易评估了，因为一旦我看到了数据，我就可以自由地进入其中进行探索和发现新事物，找出数据中可能塑造新想法的内容，并为我接下来要测试的假设生成灵感。这就是科学中许多新发现发生的地方，对吧？它不是来自于我们从现有假设或理论中测试的内容，而是当数据完全偏离轨道，把我们带向全新方向的时候。

28
00:17:39.000 --> 00:18:07.000
Like, wow, I didn't think that was going to happen. And that is super important. But it's also very important to recognize that it's more uncertain. When data go in a different direction than expected, what we would ordinarily want to do is follow up on it, develop some hypotheses and test those hypotheses for why it went in that new direction. So pre-registration helps keep clear when we're in these two different modes of research so that we can respect and value both of them.
就像：“哇，我没想到会这样。”这超级重要。但同样非常重要的是要认识到，这是更加不确定的。当数据朝着与预期不同的方向发展时，我们通常想要做的事情是跟进它、开发一些假设，并测试这些假设，弄清楚为什么它会朝那个新方向发展。因此，预注册有助于保持清晰，让我们知道自己何时处于这两种不同的研究模式中，以便我们能够尊重和珍视这两种模式。

29
00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:43.000
So if I pre-register a study and I say I'm going to conduct these 10 experiments as part of my study, does this commit me to using all of them or maybe I find that six out of the 10 confirm the hypothesis and the other four don't. But I've told everybody that I did it. I mean, can I still submit the paper and leave out those four? How committed am I to including everything? Pre-registration doesn't force you to do anything at all. It just makes it so that it's transparent what it is you did.
所以，如果我预注册了一项研究，并说我将把这10个实验作为我研究的一部分，这是否意味着我必须全部使用它们？或者也许我发现10个里面有6个证实了假设，另外4个没有。但我已经告诉了所有人我做了这些。我的意思是，我还能提交论文并把那4个漏掉吗？我对包含所有内容到底有多大程度的承诺？预注册根本不强迫你做任何事情。它只是让你的所作所为变得透明。

30
00:18:43.000 --> 00:19:12.000
So this happens all the time, right? I plan a number of studies. I pre-register them in our lab, and then a couple of them go sideways, right? Like, even just implementing the study, I had a plan for how we were going to do it, and I can't do it that way. So, like, it is a broken study. But many times it's more like what you described. Like, it just doesn't turn out the way that I thought. And so I can say, well, these are the ones I'm going to report.
所以这种情况一直都在发生，对吧？我计划了一系列研究。我们在实验室里对它们进行了预注册，然后其中有一两项进展不顺，对吧？就像，甚至在实施研究时，我有一个关于我们打算如何做的计划，但我无法按那种方式做。所以，这就成了一项失败的研究。但很多时候，情况更像是你所描述的那样，只是结果没有像我想象的那样呈现。所以我会说：“好吧，这些是我要报告的。”

31
00:19:12.000 --> 00:20:05.000
But the value of pre-registration is in those other ones are also available. They're also accessible for you as a reader or for anybody in the community to say, well, I want to, want to consider that evidence too. And so the goal of pre-registration is not to say, here are these strict rules that you now have to follow. It's here is a way to articulate and make plain what your plans were and compare that to what you did. And I can tell you why I had to make those changes. We planned to do this study with 1,0006-month-olds to measure their attention towards this versus that get into the study. Turns out it's hard to do research with six months old. We thought a thousand that was crazy. We're going to do it with 20, right, or whatever it is. And so at least I can
但预注册的价值在于，那些其他的研究也是可用的。作为读者或社区中的任何人，你也可以访问它们，说：“嗯，我也想考虑一下这些证据。”因此，预注册的目标并不是说：“这里有这些严格的规则，你现在必须遵守。”而是说：“这里有一种方法，可以阐明和说明你的计划是什么，并将其与你的实际做法进行比较。”我可以告诉你为什么我必须做出这些更改。我们计划对1000名6个月大的婴儿进行这项研究，以衡量他们对这个与那个的注意力——结果进入研究后发现，对6个月大的婴儿进行研究很难。我们觉得一千个太疯狂了，我们要对20个进行研究，或者诸如此类。所以至少我可以

32
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:52.000
then follow up and say, here's what we had to change. This is why we had to change it. And this is what we think implications it has for how confident you should be in our results. And then you as a reader get to say, hmm, I'm glad I can see that. I'm glad I can understand how it is he got from here to there, and I either agree with his explanations and the reasons that he changed or I disagree. But all of that is enabled by the transparency that pre-registration provides. It does not replace reasoning in any way. These are pretty radical changes to the way that research is being done. And I'm just wondering, are these practices catching on? Are researchers agreeing, going along and saying, yeah, you know, this is what we should have been doing all along.
