So just as one example, we operate the open science framework. This is part of what the Center for Open Science does, is this technology where researchers can use this as a collaborative management service, the OSF, where you can share your data and your materials, you can pre-register your designs, etc. The number of users of the OSF, which is open to any discipline, not just psychology, but is more than 300,000 now since it opened in 2012, right? It's been increasing in exponential rates every year. The number of registered studies is in the many, many thousands at this point. Millions of data files have been shared on the OSF.
所以仅举一个例子，我们运营着开放科学框架（Open Science Framework, OSF）。这是开放科学中心所做工作的一部分，是一项让研究人员可以将其作为协作管理服务的技术——OSF，你可以在这里分享你的数据和材料、预注册你的设计等。OSF的用户数量（对任何学科开放，不仅仅是心理学）自2012年开通以来，现在已经超过了30万人，对吧？它每年都在以指数级的速度增长。目前，注册的研究数量已经有成千上万个。数百万个数据文件已经在OSF上分享。

36
00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:55.000
And all of these show this exponential growth, and that is largely driven. by the psychological community. And I think one of the great benefits of psychology confronting this is that really the challenges that are trying to be solved are psychological challenges, right? Challenges with the individual in their behavior, challenges with the system and how individuals react to that system. And so as a consequence of psychology leading the way on a lot of this work, there's a lot of similar sorts of self-reflection and change happening in other disciplines. And so the reproducibility
所有这些都显示出这种指数级增长，而这在很大程度上是由心理学界推动的。我认为心理学正视这一问题带来的巨大好处之一是，真正试图解决的挑战是心理学挑战，对吧？个体及其行为的挑战，系统以及个体对该系统的反应的挑战。因此，随着心理学在这类工作中带头前行，许多其他学科也正在发生许多类似形式的自我反思和变革。因此，可重复性

37
00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:50.000
movement has spanned into biomedicine, into ecology, and evolutionary biology, into pre-clinical disease research, into education research, and business research. So that, I think, is going to be the real, the next 10 years is really the full embrace of these challenges across different scholarly communities. So replicating experiments that have already been conducted isn't seen as a career enhancing move in the sciences. What, if anything, is being done in an attempt to change this, to encourage people to try to reproduce studies? Yeah, replication is an important part of science because it's how we establish the reliability of findings. We rarely do a study to, to make a statement about a historical fact, like, did this thing happen or not?
运动已经扩展到了生物医学、生态学和进化生物学、临床前疾病研究、教育研究和商业研究中。因此，我认为这将是真正的未来10年——在不同的学术社群中全面拥抱这些挑战。因此，在科学界，重复已经进行过的实验并不被视为提升职业生涯的举措。为了改变这一点，鼓励人们尝试重复研究，目前正在采取什么措施（如果有的话）？是的，复制是科学的重要组成部分，因为它是我们确立发现的可靠性的方式。我们很少为了对历史事实做出陈述而进行一项研究，比如，这件事发生了还是没发生？

38
00:24:50.000 --> 00:25:23.000
That isn't usually what a scientific hypothesis or claim would be. Often scientific claims are about if these conditions occur again, then this kind of thing will happen again sometime in the future, right? We're making statements that have general application, make predictions about observations that haven't been made yet. Even if they're about events in the past, we haven't observed them yet, the hypothesis would make an anticipation of what's going to a way. occur. So replication helps with assessing whether those predictions come true when you try to do them
这通常不是科学假设或声明会做的事情。通常，科学声明是关于如果这些条件再次发生，那么未来某个时候就会再次发生这种事情，对吧？我们正在做出具有普遍应用性的陈述，对尚未进行的观察做出预测。即使它们是关于过去事件的，如果我们尚未观察到它们，假设也会对将要发生的方式做出预期。因此，复制有助于评估当你试图在与先前研究类似的语境中再次进行这些预测时，它们是否会成真。

39
00:25:23.000 --> 00:26:22.000
again in similar sorts of contexts as the previous research. But it is entirely valued because of those reward systems that we've been talking about focus on innovation and novelty at the expense of verification credibility. I gain more stature by having a phenomenon with my name on it. The Nosec phenomenon of X, right? Whereas the replication is me trying to assess the credibility of somebody else's claim, usually, could be my own too, but isn't as valued. So there are some things that are changing. One is just a growing appreciation of the role of replication in research. So journals are changing their policies to be more welcoming of replication studies, recognizing that it is important contribution to science. That opens the door. It is becoming easier to do replications
但由于我们一直在讨论的那些奖励系统专注于创新和新颖性，而牺牲了验证和可信度，它并没有得到充分的重视。我的名字如果跟某种现象联系在一起，我会获得更高的声望。比如“诺塞克X现象”，对吧？而复制则是我试图评估别人的（通常也可以是我自己的）主张的可信度，但它没有那么受重视。因此，有些事情正在改变。首先是对复制在研究中所扮演角色的认识不断提高。因此，期刊正在改变它们的政策，更加欢迎复制研究，认识到它是对科学的重要贡献。这打开了大门。复制变得越来越容易，

40
00:26:22.000 --> 00:27:02.000
because researchers are sharing their materials and their data to a greater extent. So now if someone wants to replicate somebody else's findings, they can get a leg up on figuring out how they actually did their research more quickly and easily because of greater sharing. And now there are also new publishing models that lower the barrier to trying replication studies. And one of them is called registered reports. And the idea of registered reports is to really fundamentally change how is it that researchers get that key reward, the publication. In the standard model, I do all of my research, and then I write it up and I submit it
因为研究人员在更大程度上分享他们的材料和数据。因此，现在如果有人想要复制别人的发现，由于更好的分享机制，他们可以更快、更容易地获得关于别人实际是如何进行研究的诀窍。现在也有了新的出版模式，降低了尝试复制研究的门槛。其中之一被称为“注册报告”（Registered Reports）。注册报告的想法是，从根本上改变研究人员如何获得那个关键奖励——发表。在标准模型中，我做完所有的研究，然后将其写下来并在最后提交。

41
00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:27.000
at the end. Here are all my findings. And then the peer reviewers evaluate whether it's credible and whether it should be published. In registered reports, what I submit to the journal is my plan. Here's the question that I'm going to ask. Here's the background material. Here's some initial evidence to say that this is worth studying. And here's my study design and how I plan to analyze my data when I get it.
这是我所有的发现。然后同行评议者评估它是否可信以及是否应该发表。在注册报告中，我提交给期刊的是我的计划。这是我将要提出的问题。这是背景材料。这里有一些初步证据，表明这值得研究。这是我的研究设计以及我打算如何在获得数据后对其进行分析。

42
00:27:27.000 --> 00:28:04.000
And the peer reviewers evaluate, is that an important question? And is the methodology an effective test of that question? And if they agree, then the journal commits to publishing my findings regardless of what happens. and this fundamentally changes what my goal is as the author. Instead of my goal being make the findings as flashy as exciting and as innovative as possible, my goal is ask the most important questions and design the best methods I can to test them, and that will get me the publication. And then the results are the results.
然后同行评议者评估：这是一个重要的问题吗？方法学是否是对该问题的一次有效测试？如果他们同意，那么无论结果如何，期刊都承诺发表我的发现。这从根本上改变了我作为作者的目标。我的目标不再是让发现尽可能华丽、激动人心和具有创新性，而是提出最重要的问题，并设计我能做出的最佳方法来对其进行测试，这就会为我赢得发表的机会。然后结果就是结果。

